36
00:01:09.657 --> 00:01:11.737
Right, right.
Maybe you can say like,

37
00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:14.360
"I'm still trying to kind of get it.

38
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.040
Why don't you explain it to me?"

39
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.030
Here's why I love not lying.

40
00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.640
First of all, don't lie.

41
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.500
Don't have secrets.

42
00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.410
They kind of go in the same family to me,

43
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.980
but when someone really does like something,

44
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.860
it's so exciting

45
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.610
because you know they haven't BSed you the whole time.

46
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.130
True.
And you can actually gauge

47
00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:37.290
when something is genuine versus not.

48
00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.540
Right.
So I don't think

49
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.480
that it's worth it to be fraudulent ever,

50
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.430
but I don't think that ever has to come with cruelty,

51
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.840
insensitivity or be short and curt.

52
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.600
There is a way we can tell our truth

53
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.600
that is kind, empathic, sensitive, and a little patient.

54
00:01:57.505 --> 00:01:58.640
Right, right.

55
00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.600
Thank you so much, Drew.

56
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.260
I appreciate you.
